## Description

The protocol defines a hard hop ceiling and states that routers must enforce it: `packages/network/src/protocol.ts:135` declares `export const MAX_TTL = 32;` with the comment "Routers MUST reject messages with ttl > MAX_TTL." The router never applies it. `MessageRouter.route` only rejects a TTL that is already exhausted, then decrements and forwards:

```ts
// packages/network/src/message-router.ts:173-193
async route(message: AgentMessage): Promise<RoutingResult> {
  // ── 1. TTL check ──
  if (message.routing.ttl <= 0) {
    return this.fail(message, "dropped_ttl", ProtocolErrorCode.TTL_EXPIRED, "Message TTL expired");
  }
  // ── 2. Record this hop ──
  const hop: RouteHop = { agentId: this.agentId, timestamp: new Date().toISOString() };
  const forwardMsg: AgentMessage = {
    ...message,
    routing: { ...message.routing, ttl: message.routing.ttl - 1, path: [...message.routing.path, hop] },
  };
```

`MAX_TTL` is imported nowhere in `message-router.ts`. In addition, although every hop is appended to `routing.path` (line 191), the router never inspects that path to detect that the local agent already appears in it, so it will happily re-forward a message that loops back through it.

## Impact

A peer (which is currently unauthenticated — see the handshake findings) can craft a message with an arbitrarily large `ttl` (e.g. millions). Each router decrements by one and forwards, so the message can traverse up to that many hops, and with a routing cycle (A→B→A…) it bounces until the inflated TTL drains — amplifying a single injected message into a large volume of inter-agent traffic. This is a routing-loop / traffic-amplification denial-of-service vector that the protocol explicitly intended `MAX_TTL` to prevent. Severity **high**: remotely triggerable resource amplification across the agent mesh.

## Required Fix

1. At the top of `route`, reject (or clamp) any message whose `routing.ttl` exceeds `MAX_TTL`, emitting a `TTL_EXPIRED`/`MALFORMED_MESSAGE` protocol error, per the protocol contract.
2. Add loop detection: if `message.routing.path` already contains `this.agentId`, drop the message instead of forwarding it.
3. Add tests for `ttl > MAX_TTL` rejection and for a cyclic `via`/route-table configuration that must terminate.

## References

- CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html
- CWE-834: Excessive Iteration — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/834.html
- OWASP API Security Top 10 2023 API4: Unrestricted Resource Consumption — https://owasp.org/API-Security/editions/2023/en/0xa4-unrestricted-resource-consumption/

## Description

The package ships a structural header validator, `validateHeaders` (`packages/network/src/protocol.ts:146-175`), which checks `messageId`, `type`, `protocolVersion` (valid semver), `createdAt`, and `metadata`. The message router never calls it. `MessageRouter.route` consumes `message.routing.*` and `message.headers.messageId` directly and forwards based solely on the TTL and destination, with no validation of the inbound envelope:

```ts
// packages/network/src/message-router.ts:194-210
// ── 3. Are we the destination? ──
if (message.routing.destination === this.agentId) {
  return { outcome: "delivered", deliveredTo: this.agentId };
}
// ── 4. Determine next hop ──
const nextHop = this.resolveNextHop(forwardMsg);
if (!nextHop) {
  return this.fail(forwardMsg, "no_route", ProtocolErrorCode.NO_ROUTE, `No route to "${forwardMsg.routing.destination}"`);
}
```

`validateHeaders` is not imported in `message-router.ts` (it is only exported from the package index). There is also no validation that `routing.source`, `routing.destination`, `routing.ttl`, `routing.via`, or `routing.path` are well-formed before they are read and propagated.

## Impact

Malformed or forged messages are routed without scrutiny: a message with a missing/garbage `messageId`, an invalid `protocolVersion`, a non-array `via`/`path`, or a non-numeric `ttl` flows through the router. At best this causes runtime errors when fields are dereferenced; at worst it lets an attacker push structurally-invalid envelopes deep into the mesh and into downstream handlers that assume the schema has been validated. The protocol provides `validateHeaders` and the `INVALID_HEADER` error code precisely for this gate, but the router bypasses it. Severity **high** because the router is the choke point that every inbound message passes through.

## Required Fix

1. Call `validateHeaders(message.headers)` at the start of `route`; on any errors, return a `fail(...)` with `ProtocolErrorCode.INVALID_HEADER` (or `MALFORMED_MESSAGE`) instead of forwarding.
2. Validate the `routing` block as well (string `source`/`destination`, array `via`/`path`, finite non-negative `ttl`) before reading those fields.
3. Add tests asserting that messages with invalid headers/routing are rejected rather than forwarded.

## References

- CWE-20: Improper Input Validation — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html
- OWASP API Security Top 10 2023 API8: Security Misconfiguration — https://owasp.org/API-Security/editions/2023/en/0xa8-security-misconfiguration/

## Description

`PromptTracker` caps usage records via `maxUsageRecords` (FIFO `shift()`), but the parallel `outcomeRecords` array has no cap and is only ever appended to:

```ts
// packages/learning/src/prompt-tracker.ts:49-54
private readonly usageRecords: PromptUsageRecord[] = [];
private readonly outcomeRecords: PromptOutcomeRecord[] = [];
private readonly maxUsageRecords: number;
constructor(config: PromptTrackerConfig = {}) {
  this.maxUsageRecords = config.maxUsageRecords ?? 50_000;
}
```

```ts
// packages/learning/src/prompt-tracker.ts:94 (recordOutcome)
this.outcomeRecords.push(record);
return record;
```

There is no `maxOutcomeRecords` config and no `outcomeRecords.shift()`/eviction anywhere in the class. Every call to `recordOutcome` permanently retains another `PromptOutcomeRecord`.

## Impact

In a long-running process that continuously scores prompt outcomes, `outcomeRecords` grows without bound, leaking memory and progressively slowing `getVersionStats` (which iterates the entire array on every call). Because outcomes are recorded for every scored interaction, this leak is driven by normal traffic, not an edge case. Severity **high**: unbounded growth in a hot, always-on optimization path eventually degrades or crashes the process. (Note: the usage side is correctly capped, which makes the asymmetry a clear oversight rather than an intentional unbounded store.)

## Required Fix

1. Add a `maxOutcomeRecords` config (with a sane default) and evict oldest outcomes FIFO in `recordOutcome`, mirroring the existing `maxUsageRecords` logic.
2. Alternatively, maintain incremental per-version aggregates (count + running mean) so raw outcome records need not be retained at all.
3. Add a test asserting `outcomeRecords` length stays bounded under sustained `recordOutcome` calls.

## References

- CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html
- CWE-401: Missing Release of Memory after Effective Lifetime — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/401.html

## Description

Note: the original finding described eviction happening inside `recordOutcome`; on inspection the FIFO eviction is in `recordUsage`, and the data-loss surfaces in `recordOutcome`. The defect itself is real and is documented accordingly.

`recordUsage` enforces the `maxUsageRecords` cap by dropping the oldest usage record:

```ts
// packages/learning/src/prompt-tracker.ts:65-71
this.usageRecords.push(record);
if (this.usageRecords.length > this.maxUsageRecords) {
  this.usageRecords.shift();
}
return record;
```

`recordOutcome` later looks the usage record up by id and throws if it is gone:

```ts
// packages/learning/src/prompt-tracker.ts:78-82
recordOutcome(usageId: string, score: number): PromptOutcomeRecord {
  const usage = this.usageRecords.find((r) => r.id === usageId);
  if (!usage) {
    throw new Error(`Usage record '${usageId}' not found`);
  }
```

When a usage record is evicted (which is expected behaviour once the cap is reached) and its score arrives afterwards — a normal occurrence, since scoring is asynchronous and may lag usage — `recordOutcome` throws instead of degrading gracefully. The outcome score is lost, and an unhandled exception is raised into the scoring caller.

## Impact

In a busy tracker that has reached `maxUsageRecords`, late-arriving scores for recently-evicted usages cause `recordOutcome` to throw, dropping legitimate outcome data and risking an uncaught error in the caller's scoring path. The optimization loop (`PromptOptimizer`) silently loses signal, and callers that do not wrap `recordOutcome` in try/catch can crash. Severity **medium**: it corrupts the learning signal and turns an expected eviction race into a hard failure.

## Required Fix

1. Make `recordOutcome` tolerant of a missing usage record: skip (and optionally log/return null) instead of throwing, so a late score for an evicted usage is dropped without raising.
2. If the templateKey/version can be supplied directly by the caller, allow recording the outcome without requiring the usage record to still be present.
3. Add a test that evicts a usage via the cap and then calls `recordOutcome` for it, asserting no throw.

## References

- CWE-755: Improper Handling of Exceptional Conditions — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/755.html

## Description

`ContextSerializer.merge` deduplicates memory entries by `id`, but performs no deduplication of conversation history — remote messages are unconditionally prepended:

```ts
// packages/network/src/context-serializer.ts:168-180
static merge(local: AgentContext, remote: SerializedAgentContext): AgentContext {
  const remoteMessages = remote.conversationHistory.map(deserializeMessage);
  const remoteMemory = remote.memory.map(deserializeMemoryEntry);

  const localMemoryIds = new Set(local.memory.map((m) => m.id));
  const newMemory = remoteMemory.filter((m) => !localMemoryIds.has(m.id));

  return {
    ...local,
    conversationHistory: [...remoteMessages, ...local.conversationHistory],
    memory: [...newMemory, ...local.memory],
  };
}
```

If the same snapshot (or two snapshots covering overlapping windows) is merged into a context that already contains those messages, every overlapping message is appended again. Memory is protected from this by the id-based filter on lines 172-173; conversation history has no equivalent guard.

## Impact

Repeated or overlapping context hand-offs duplicate conversation turns in the merged context. Duplicated history inflates token usage on the next model call, can confuse the agent with repeated user/assistant turns, and grows unboundedly under repeated merges. Severity **medium**: it silently corrupts the conversational state that downstream reasoning depends on. (`SerializedMessage` carries no stable id, so dedup must key on content+role+timestamp.)

## Required Fix

1. Deduplicate `conversationHistory` during merge using a stable key (e.g. `role|timestamp|content`, or introduce a message id), dropping remote messages already present locally.
2. Document and test the idempotency expectation: merging the same snapshot twice must not change the result.

## References

- CWE-694: Use of Multiple Resources with Duplicate Identifier — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/694.html

## Description

`PromptOptimizer.optimize` automatically promotes whichever template version has the highest mean outcome score, driven solely by the scores held in `PromptTracker`:

```ts
// packages/learning/src/prompt-optimizer.ts:114-126
// Only consider versions with enough data
const qualified = allStats.filter(
  (s) => s.scoredCount >= this.minSampleSize && s.meanScore !== null
);
if (qualified.length === 0) { skippedKeys.push(`${key} (insufficient sample size)`); continue; }
// Find the best performing version
const best = qualified.reduce((prev, cur) =>
  (cur.meanScore ?? 0) > (prev.meanScore ?? 0) ? cur : prev
);
```

```ts
// packages/learning/src/prompt-optimizer.ts:156-157
// Promote the winner
this.registry.promote(key, best.version);
```

The only promotion guards are `minSampleSize`, `minImprovementDelta`, and an opt-in `safetyCriticalKeys` set. `PromptTracker.recordOutcome` accepts any caller-supplied `score` with no authentication, attribution, or anomaly checks, and the optimizer treats those scores as ground truth and mutates the live active template version.

## Impact

If any component that can call `recordOutcome` is compromised or fed attacker-influenced feedback, an adversary can inflate the mean score of a chosen (e.g. malicious or low-quality) template version past `minImprovementDelta` and have it auto-promoted into production for all traffic on that key — a score/feedback-poisoning attack that lets an attacker control the prompts the system actually uses. Templates not explicitly tagged safety-critical have no protection. Severity **medium**: exploitation requires influence over the outcome-scoring path, but the consequence (silent promotion of attacker-chosen prompts) is significant.

## Required Fix

1. Require human (or signed) approval before a promotion takes effect by default — `optimize()` should produce promotion *candidates*; only an authorized step applies `registry.promote`.
2. Authenticate/attribute outcome scores and add anomaly detection (e.g. reject implausible score distributions, cap per-source influence) before they feed promotion.
3. Make the safety gate default-deny (promotion off unless a key is explicitly opted in) rather than relying on an opt-out `safetyCriticalKeys` list.
4. Add tests demonstrating that a flood of high scores from one source cannot unilaterally trigger a promotion.

## References

- CWE-345: Insufficient Verification of Data Authenticity — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/345.html
- CWE-807: Reliance on Untrusted Inputs in a Security Decision — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/807.html
- OWASP Machine Learning Security Top 10 — ML02 Data Poisoning Attack — https://owasp.org/www-project-machine-learning-security-top-10/

## Description

Each dashboard's patch array is correctly bounded as a ring buffer, but the `buffer` Map that holds one array per `dashboardId` is only ever added to (never pruned except by `destroy()`):

```ts
// packages/ui/src/dashboard-streamer.ts:72
private readonly buffer = new Map<string, DashboardPatch[]>(); // dashboardId → patches
```

```ts
// packages/ui/src/dashboard-streamer.ts:202-211 (dispatch)
private dispatch(patch: DashboardPatch): void {
  // Buffer patch (ring buffer)
  if (!this.buffer.has(patch.dashboardId)) {
    this.buffer.set(patch.dashboardId, []);
  }
  const buf = this.buffer.get(patch.dashboardId)!;
  buf.push(patch);
  if (buf.length > this.bufferSize) {
    buf.splice(0, buf.length - this.bufferSize);
  }
```

`unsubscribe` (`packages/ui/src/dashboard-streamer.ts:107-116`) removes handler/token entries and deletes the dashboard's handler map when empty, but never deletes the corresponding `buffer` entry. So once any patch has been dispatched for a dashboard id, its (bufferSize-capped) array is retained indefinitely, even after all subscribers leave and the dashboard is gone.

## Impact

In a deployment that creates many short-lived or unique dashboard ids (per-session dashboards, per-user views, etc.), the `buffer` Map accumulates one retained array per distinct id for the lifetime of the streamer, leaking memory proportional to the number of dashboards ever seen rather than the number currently active. The per-dashboard ring bound prevents each entry from growing, but does not bound the count of entries. Severity **medium**: a gradual unbounded leak driven by dashboard-id cardinality.

## Required Fix

1. Delete the `buffer` entry for a dashboard when its last subscriber unsubscribes (in `unsubscribe`, alongside the existing `handlers.delete`).
2. Alternatively/additionally, add an idle-eviction or LRU cap on the number of buffered dashboards, and prune buffers that have had no subscribers for a configurable interval.
3. Add a test asserting the `buffer` Map shrinks after the last subscriber for a dashboard unsubscribes.

## References

- CWE-401: Missing Release of Memory after Effective Lifetime — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/401.html
- CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html

## Description

When profiling object-array columns, `DataAnalyzer` classifies date columns using `numericThreshold` — the config option documented strictly as the threshold for *numeric* columns:

```ts
// packages/ui/src/data-analyzer.ts:198-204
const numericKeys = keys.filter(
  (k) => profiles[k].numeric / rows.length >= this.numericThreshold
);
const dateKeys = keys.filter((k) => profiles[k].date / rows.length >= this.numericThreshold);
const stringKeys = keys.filter(
  (k) => profiles[k].string / rows.length >= this.numericThreshold
);
```

`numericThreshold` is documented as "Minimum fraction of rows where a column is **numeric** before the analyzer treats it as a numeric column" (`packages/ui/src/data-analyzer.ts:108-114`). Reusing it for the `date` fraction means there is no independent control over date detection, and the same threshold is overloaded across three different type classifications.

## Impact

Date-column detection is governed by an option whose name and documentation refer only to numeric columns, so tuning `numericThreshold` silently and surprisingly changes timeline/date-axis recommendations (`recommendedKind: "timeline"` and the `line` chart-type branch both depend on `dateKeys`). Columns with a mixed-but-mostly-date profile that falls below the numeric threshold are dropped from `dateKeys`, causing the analyzer to misclassify timelines as plain tables/charts. Severity **medium**: incorrect widget recommendations and a misleading configuration contract, though it is a UX/correctness bug rather than a security issue.

## Required Fix

1. Introduce a dedicated `dateThreshold` config option (defaulting to the same value for backward compatibility) and use it for the `dateKeys` filter.
2. Optionally add a `stringThreshold` so each type classification is independently controllable, and update the documentation accordingly.
3. Add tests covering a column whose date fraction sits between distinct numeric and date thresholds.

## References

- Internal: `DataAnalyzerConfig.numericThreshold` documentation at `packages/ui/src/data-analyzer.ts:108-114`.

## Description

Note: the original finding listed this under `packages/ui`; the only `interpolate`/`PromptComposer` implementation is in the **learning** package (`packages/learning/src/prompt-composer.ts`), so it is filed under `area:learning`.

`interpolate` substitutes `{{variable}}` placeholders with the stringified context value, with no escaping, length limit, or sanitization:

```ts
// packages/learning/src/prompt-composer.ts:47-52
function interpolate(template: string, context: Record<string, unknown>): string {
  return template.replace(/\{\{(\w+)\}\}/g, (match, key: string) => {
    const value = context[key];
    return value !== undefined ? String(value) : match;
  });
}
```

This function is the substitution engine behind `compose`, `composeSingle`, and `render` (`packages/learning/src/prompt-composer.ts:89,92,122`). The injected values (`userName`, `language`, task metadata, etc.) typically derive from user-controlled input, and they are spliced directly into the prompt that is later sent to the model.

## Impact

If any interpolated value is attacker-influenced, the attacker can embed instructions or delimiter sequences (e.g. `Ignore previous instructions...`, fake system/role markers, or nested `{{...}}` payloads) that become part of the composed prompt. This is a prompt-injection vector through template variables: the composer offers no neutralization, so untrusted data and trusted instructions are concatenated without a boundary. Severity **medium**: impact depends on what the composed prompt drives, but the substitution layer is a natural and currently-undefended choke point.

## Required Fix

1. Sanitize interpolated values before substitution: strip/escape control characters and known injection markers, and cap value length.
2. Route untrusted values through the project's existing input-validation/injection-detection utilities (e.g. the `security` package's validator) rather than embedding raw `String(value)`.
3. Ensure substituted values cannot themselves introduce new `{{...}}` placeholders that a later pass would expand (single-pass substitution / escape braces in values).
4. Add tests with values containing injection phrases and brace sequences asserting they are neutralized.

## References

- OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications 2025 — LLM01: Prompt Injection — https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/
- CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection') — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html

## Description

`buildSpec` switches over `WidgetKind` with no `default` branch and no exhaustiveness guard:

```ts
// packages/ui/src/auto-widgets.ts:121-160 (abridged)
switch (kind) {
  case "table": { /* ... */ return tableTemplate(...); }
  case "chart": { /* ... */ return chartTemplate(...); }
  case "list": { /* ... */ return listTemplate(...); }
  case "card": { /* ... */ return cardTemplate(...); }
  case "metric": { /* ... */ return metricTemplate(...); }
  case "form": { /* ... */ return formTemplate(...); }
  case "timeline": { /* ... */ return timelineTemplate(...); }
}
// (no default, no `return` after the switch)
```

The seven `case`s cover the current `WidgetKind` union (`packages/ui/src/widget-templates.ts:14`), so the function compiles under `noImplicitReturns`. However, `kind` originates from `hints.kind ?? analysis.recommendedKind` (`packages/ui/src/auto-widgets.ts:86`), and `hints.kind` comes from caller-supplied `AutoWidgetHints`. A value outside the union (passed from untyped JS, a cast, or a future kind added to the type but not the switch) falls through every case and the function returns `undefined` while still typed as `WidgetSpec`.

## Impact

An out-of-type or newly-added `kind` produces a `WidgetSpec` that is actually `undefined`, deferring the failure to whatever consumes the spec (rendering/serialization) where it manifests as a confusing `TypeError` far from the cause. There is no compile-time `assertNever` to catch a future union member that is added without a corresponding case. Severity **low**: it is a robustness/maintainability gap rather than an exploitable defect, since well-typed call sites are currently exhaustive.

## Required Fix

1. Add a `default` branch that either throws a descriptive error (e.g. `Unsupported widget kind: ${kind}`) or assigns to a `const _exhaustive: never = kind;` to force a compile error when `WidgetKind` grows.
2. Validate `hints.kind` against the known set before dispatch so invalid runtime input is rejected with a clear message.
3. Add a test passing an unknown kind and asserting a clear error rather than an `undefined` spec.

## References

- CWE-478: Missing Default Case in Multiple Condition Expression — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/478.html

## Description

The protocol module provides `checkCompatibility(localPV, messagePV)` and `parseSemVer` (`packages/network/src/protocol.ts:57-61`) to decide whether two protocol versions are compatible per SemVer rules. `handleConfirm` does not use them; it compares the negotiated version with a plain string equality:

```ts
// packages/network/src/handshake.ts:349-358
// Verify the negotiated version matches what we agreed on
if (confirm.negotiatedVersion && confirm.negotiatedVersion !== session.protocolVersion) {
  session.status = "rejected";
  session.updatedAt = new Date();
  return {
    step: "REJECT",
    reason: `Version mismatch in CONFIRM: expected ${session.protocolVersion} got ${confirm.negotiatedVersion}`,
    errorCode: ProtocolErrorCode.VERSION_MISMATCH,
  };
}
```

Elsewhere the package treats version handling semantically via `negotiateVersion`/`checkCompatibility`, but this final confirmation step reduces it to byte-for-byte string matching and does not validate that `confirm.negotiatedVersion` is even a well-formed semver.

## Impact

The check is inconsistent with the rest of the protocol layer: any cosmetically-different but compatible version string (e.g. differing formatting, or a value the negotiation logic would accept) is rejected, and a malformed `negotiatedVersion` is never caught by `parseSemVer`. In practice `SUPPORTED_VERSIONS` currently holds a single value (`1.0.0`), so the divergence is latent today, but it becomes a real interoperability bug as soon as more versions exist. Severity **low**: behavioural inconsistency / latent bug rather than an active failure.

## Required Fix

1. Parse `confirm.negotiatedVersion` with `parseSemVer` and reject if it is not valid semver.
2. Use `checkCompatibility` (or re-run `negotiateVersion` semantics) against `session.protocolVersion` instead of `!==`, so the CONFIRM step accepts the same versions the rest of the handshake does.
3. Add tests covering a compatible-but-non-identical version and a malformed version string.

## References

- Internal: `checkCompatibility`/`parseSemVer` at `packages/network/src/protocol.ts:24-61`.
